{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>I will guarantee him, etc., and I will have sinned, etc., all the days.</b> The reason he said ״all the days״ — our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 91:10) said: the World to Come, which is entirely days." "And in the chapter HaGolin (Makkot 11b), Rav Yehudah said: a ban imposed on condition requires release. From where do we know this? From Yehudah, as it is written ״if I do not bring him״ etc. And Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani said in the name of Rabbi Yochanan: what is the meaning of what is written ״let Reuben live״ etc.? All those forty years that Israel were in the wilderness, the bones of Yehudah were rolling about until Moshe stood and etc. — end of quote. And if you would say that perhaps Yehudah's punishment was because he did not double the condition like the condition of the sons of Gad etc. (Kiddushin 61b), so that the act takes effect in any case —" "For this reason I have seen that the Gaon Rabbi Eliyahu Mizrahi explained that in this matter Yehudah was not mistaken, nor was his father; but a ban upon a condition that requires release — it is possible that Yehudah erred, and his father too, because this law was not known except from the incident that happened to Yehudah etc., unlike the doubling of a condition which is explicit in the Torah. His words are wondrous to me, for whichever way you turn it: if the Torah as written was not known to the Patriarchs, then from where would they know that the sons of Gad and the sons of Reuben asked for land across the Jordan and Moshe stipulated with them a doubled condition? And if they did know all the halakhot heard from the Torah of Moshe, why would this particular halakhah be lacking so that they did not know it? And you will find that they said in tractate Chullin that all the halakhot were stated at Sinai, except that each was repeated in its place — like the sinew of the thigh (gid haNasheh), which although it was mentioned in the portion of Vayishlach in the matter of Yaakov, they were commanded concerning it at Sinai but it was written in its place, and likewise all the halakhot. And if so, this law learned from Yehudah was likewise stated at Sinai, and from now on there is no difference between the halakhot whether they are learned in this order or in that order; and when we say that Yaakov knew everything, just as he knew this one, he knew that one." "<b>Further,</b> something greater than this I see: for this law of a doubled condition — they disputed it, for Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel maintains that one need not double the condition, and the reasoning of the sons of Gad and the sons of Reuben follows the opinions stated there in the Gemara; whereas the law of a ban upon a condition is an agreed-upon halakhah, and no one disputes it. And if so, on the contrary, there is more reason to attribute Yehudah's punishment to his not having doubled the condition, since he held according to the opinion of Rabbi Chanina ben Gamliel, and likewise Yaakov held. And since the halakhah is not so, his bones were rolling in the ark, because it was said that a ban upon a condition requires release — a simple halakhah — and Yaakov and his sons erred in it." "<b>But the correct interpretation</b> is that the intent of the words of Rav Yehudah, who said: a ban upon a condition requires release — is in this manner: if one imposed a ban on condition and did not double it by saying ״and if not — I am not under ban,״ this is what requires release; but if he doubled and negated — it does not require release. And of a similar nature he also said there in the Talmud (Makkot 11b): Rabbi Abbahu said: the curse of a sage, even if it is on condition, takes effect — from where do we know this? From Eli etc." "And if you say: what need is there for Rebbi Yehudah to make this known to us? A Mishnah (Kiddushin 3:4) is already arranged: ״Any condition that is not like the condition of…״ — the deed stands.״ This is not so, for regarding vows and oaths the essence is the heart, as per the statement of Rebbi Akiva who expounded (Shevuot 26a) ״הָאָדָם בִּשְׁבוּעָה״ — that his heart and his mouth must be equivalent, and without this it is not an oath, and the same holds for vows and bans of excommunication. From this one might say that in a ban of excommunication, even though he did not stipulate a double condition, since his heart did not resolve to ban except if it would be so, why should he be bound by something that was not so in his heart? And the Torah said ״הָאָדָם בִּשְׁבוּעָה.״ Therefore Rebbi Yehudah informed us that had he not doubled it, even though he made it a ban on condition, it would require release. And there is no proof for this law except from Yehudah. And the reason for this law is like the reason for the law of a sage's curse mentioned nearby, and we have nothing except what Scripture revealed to us in the episode of Yehudah and in the episode of Eli, and also the episode of Yaakov who cursed (Genesis 31:32) ״עִם אֲשֶׁר וְגוֹ׳ לֹא יִחְיֶה״, and in all of them there was no negation of the matter through doubling the condition." "<b>And what</b> the Rav, Rebbi Eliyahu Mizrachi, wrote — that the doubling is stated [orally] but not written — we have already rejected those words and there is no need to add to reject them." "<b>Again</b>, I saw the Tosafot who wrote in explanation of the words of Rebbi Yehudah, that the reason the ban of excommunication of Yehudah took effect, even though it was on condition, is because it was not within his power to fulfill it, and therefore it took effect even on the condition, etc.; but other conditions that are within his power to fulfill — such as when one bans a person not to do a certain thing — the ban certainly does not take effect at all. Thus far [their words]." "<b>Now according to the words of the Tosafot,</b> even though the one banning doubled the condition, it nevertheless takes effect upon him because it is not within his power to fulfill it, and this is the opposite of what we wrote. And I do not know how they would interpret the statement of Rebbi Abbahu immediately following it, who said: a sage's curse even if on condition, and he proves it from Eli, who said to Shmuel ״כֹּה יַעֲשֶׂה לְךָ וְגוֹ׳ אִם תְּכַחֵד״, and the matter was in his power to tell him immediately, and it was in Shmuel's power to tell him. And it can be resolved. And do not raise the difficulty: but the words of Rebbi Yehudah were stated unqualifiedly — a ban on condition — and he did not distinguish between a matter he is able to do and a matter, etc.; for he relied on the proof and judged from it as it is and as it presents itself. Also according to what I explained, that Rebbi Yehudah's intent was regarding the doubling, do not be concerned that Rebbi Yehudah erred, for he relied on what the text teaches from it, and in it there was no doubling." "<b>Further,</b> I looked into the words of the Tosafot and saw that they hold, as mentioned above, that one who bans regarding a matter that is within his power to do, even though he did not stipulate a double condition — does not require release. If so, if it were not for the reason that the ban took effect upon Yehudah because it was not within his power to fulfill it, even though he did not double the condition — the deed would not stand and a double condition would not be needed, and not like the words of the Rav, Rebbi Eliyahu Mizrachi. We thus find two reasons in the law of one who bans on condition: the first, on the side that he did not double, is that it requires release, from the law of a double condition; and the second, on the side that he banned regarding a matter not within his authority, and that is the opinion of the Tosafot. And what remains for us to know is why Yehudah did not release his ban." "<b>And I saw</b> the Ra'avad, of blessed memory, in his glosses on the Rambam in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Talmud Torah, where he objects to what the Rambam wrote there: a scholar who excommunicated himself, even if he excommunicated himself with the consent of his fellow, even for a matter that obligates excommunication — he may absolve himself, end quote. He objected: why then did Judah not absolve himself? And beyond all this, it is difficult for me: why did Jacob not absolve him? End." "<b>And I saw</b> the Rashba, who wrote that what a sage is able to do — to absolve his own excommunication — is specifically when it is not for something that obligates excommunication, as in the case of Mar Zutra and so forth. And according to the Rashba's approach, the only difficulty is the question of Jacob — why he did not absolve him; but Judah cannot [absolve himself]. Yet in any case it should have been possible for Judah to gather three ordinary men and absolve himself, and there is no element of ״with the consent of his fellow״ here that would require [Jacob's] consent, since the obligation that bound him — to bring Benjamin before him — had already dissolved. Furthermore, he should have informed him, and it is obvious that he would not wish to cause pain to Judah his son for nothing. And the matter is straightforward to me, for in any case there is no difficulty concerning Jacob: when we say that he knew the law that absolution is required, he relied on the fact that Judah would absolve himself through others — his brothers and the like — and there is no difficulty concerning Jacob. Only concerning Judah do we need to investigate to understand his reasoning." "<b>Now behold,</b> according to what I have proposed in explaining the words of Rav Yehudah — that the reason absolution was required is that he did not double the condition — according to this, Judah's reasoning was that he held like Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel, that doubling the condition is not required, and in this he erred. But one may ask: why did they not prove to Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel from Judah, who was punished, that the words of Rav Yehudah — as we have established them regarding the law of doubling the condition — are unlike Rabbi Hanina ben Gamliel? And according to the reasoning of the Tosafot, who explained the words of Rav Yehudah to mean that the reason he requires absolution of the excommunication is because he imposed it for a matter not within his control, one must say that this law was hidden from Judah's eyes, and Jacob — even though it is said that he knew it — relied on the fact that Judah would absolve himself on his own."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:05.036671434Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}